  { n:21, summary:"Naboth the Jezreelite owns a vineyard beside Ahab's palace in Jezreel, and Ahab wants it for a garden of herbs, offering money or a better vineyard. Naboth refuses to give the inheritance of his fathers, and Ahab goes home heavy, lays on his bed, and will not eat. Jezebel writes letters in Ahab's name to the elders of Jezreel, ordering a fast, Naboth set on high, and two worthless men to witness that he blasphemed God and the king. Naboth is carried out and stoned. When Ahab rises to take possession of the vineyard, the word of the Lord sends Elijah to meet him: Hast thou killed, and also taken possession? Because Ahab sold himself to work evil — stirred up by Jezebel, none like him for wickedness — his house will be like Jeroboam's and Baasha's, the dogs will eat Jezebel by the wall of Jezreel, and dogs and birds will consume his posterity. Ahab rents his clothes, puts on sackcloth, and fasts; because he humbles himself, the Lord defers the evil to his son's days.",
    context:"In Jezreel, Jezebel has Naboth falsely accused and stoned so Ahab can take his vineyard, and Elijah meets the king with the Lord's judgment.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Kings 21:3", text:"And Naboth said to Ahab, The LORD forbid it me, that I should give the inheritance of my fathers unto thee." },
      { ref:"1 Kings 21:19", text:"And thou shalt speak unto him, saying, Thus saith the LORD, Hast thou killed, and also taken possession? And thou shalt speak unto him, saying, Thus saith the LORD, In the place where dogs licked the blood of Naboth shall dogs lick thy blood, even thine." },
      { ref:"1 Kings 21:29", text:"Seest thou how Ahab humbleth himself before me? because he humbleth himself before me, I will not bring the evil in his days: but in his son's days will I bring the evil upon his house." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord defends the defenseless against abuse of power; humble repentance can defer judgment, though consequences remain.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did Naboth refuse to sell his vineyard to Ahab?", choices:["It belonged to the temple","The LORD forbade giving away the inheritance of his fathers","He planned to build on it","Jezebel had threatened him first"], a:1, why:"Naboth said, The LORD forbid it me, that I should give the inheritance of my fathers unto thee." },
      { q:"How did Jezebel secure Naboth's death?", choices:["She poisoned him at a feast","She paid his servants to kill him","She wrote letters in Ahab's name; two false witnesses accused him of blasphemy, and he was stoned","She banished him to Syria"], a:2, why:"Jezebel sealed letters with Ahab's seal to the elders of Jezreel; two sons of Belial bore false witness that he blasphemed God and the king, and Naboth was stoned." },
      { q:"What changed when Ahab humbled himself after Elijah's rebuke?", choices:["The Lord canceled every consequence","Jezebel repented and returned the vineyard","Ahab restored Naboth's family","The Lord deferred the promised evil to the days of Ahab's son"], a:3, why:"Because Ahab humbled himself before the Lord, the evil was not brought in his days, but in his son's days." }
    ] },
  { n:22, summary:"After three years of peace between Syria and Israel, Ahab invites Jehoshaphat of Judah to help retake Ramoth-gilead, and Jehoshaphat asks to inquire of the Lord. About four hundred prophets, Zedekiah among them with horns of iron, promise victory; Ahab admits there remains one more, Micaiah the son of Imlah, whom he hates because he never prophesies good concerning him. Adjured to tell the truth, Micaiah describes Israel scattered as sheep without a shepherd, and a vision of the Lord's throne sending a lying spirit into the mouths of Ahab's prophets. Slapped by Zedekiah and imprisoned on bread and water of affliction, Micaiah answers, If thou return at all in peace, the LORD hath not spoken by me. Ahab, disguised while Jehoshaphat wears his robes, is smitten by an arrow drawn at a venture, dies at even, and is buried in Samaria, his blood licked by dogs according to the word of the Lord. Ahaziah his son reigns in Israel, serving Baal; the chapter closes reviewing Jehoshaphat's good reign in Judah.",
    context:"Ahab and Jehoshaphat of Judah go up against Ramoth-gilead despite Micaiah's warning, and Ahab dies in battle as the word of the Lord said.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Kings 22:14", text:"And Micaiah said, As the LORD liveth, what the LORD saith unto me, that will I speak." },
      { ref:"1 Kings 22:28", text:"And Micaiah said, If thou return at all in peace, the LORD hath not spoken by me. And he said, Hearken, O people, every one of you." },
      { ref:"1 Kings 22:34", text:"And a certain man drew a bow at a venture, and smote the king of Israel between the joints of the harness: wherefore he said unto the driver of his chariot, Turn thine hand, and carry me out of the host; for I am wounded." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Seek the Lord's true word even when it is unwelcome; ignoring a true warning does not stop its fulfillment.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who was the one prophet Ahab said could be asked about the battle, and why did Ahab dislike him?", choices:["Elijah; he had announced a drought","Ahijah; he had rent a garment","Micaiah the son of Imlah; he never prophesied good concerning Ahab, but evil","Obadiah; he had hidden the prophets"], a:2, why:"Ahab told Jehoshaphat there is yet one man, Micaiah the son of Imlah, but I hate him; for he doth not prophesy good concerning me, but evil." },
      { q:"What did Micaiah's vision reveal about Ahab's four hundred prophets?", choices:["They spoke by the Spirit of the Lord","They were prophets of Baal hired by Jezebel","They had never truly prophesied before","A lying spirit had been put in their mouths, and the Lord had spoken evil concerning Ahab"], a:3, why:"Micaiah saw the LORD on His throne and a spirit offering to be a lying spirit in the mouth of all Ahab's prophets; the Lord said, Thou shalt persuade him, and prevail." },
      { q:"How was Ahab mortally wounded at Ramoth-gilead?", choices:["A certain man drew a bow at a venture and smote him between the joints of the harness","Zedekiah's men speared him at the gate","Jehoshaphat's chariot struck his","He fell from his horse fleeing the battle"], a:0, why:"A man drew a bow at a venture, smiting the disguised king between the joints of his harness; he was propped up in his chariot and died at even." }
    ] }
